.TH GPS-TOF 1 "2026-11-05" "gps-tof 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gps-tof \- straight-line time of flight between surveyed sites with a correction ledger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gps-tof
[\fB\-c\fR \fICONFIG\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fISPEC\fR]... [\fB\-p\fR \fIFROM:TO[:LABEL[:AXIS]]\fR]... [\fB\-\-nmea\fR \fIFILE\fR]...
[\fB\-f\fR text|md|json|all] [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR] [\fIsetting overrides\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gps-tof
converts an arbitrary set of site coordinates into Earth-centred positions, computes the
Euclidean chord between every requested pair of sites, and reports the time of flight at
\fIc\fR in seconds, in clock ticks and in a legacy unit.  For every baseline the report
contains:
.IP \(bu 2
a \fBcorrection ledger\fR: the reference (spherical) model, the chosen ellipsoid, the
height-datum conversion of each site (MSL to ellipsoidal), and the vertical and horizontal
antenna-to-endpoint offsets of each site, each with its change in length, time and ticks;
.IP \(bu 2
fixed \fBtiming shifts\fR (for example an accelerator timing-reference change expressed in
machine turns) and the combined offset, exact and rounded;
.IP \(bu 2
an optional \fBtrigger window\fR in ticks;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBchord geometry\fR: central angle, arc minus chord, sagitta, dip and rise angles, azimuths,
and the off-axis angle with respect to a reference axis;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnon-geometric effects\fR: Sagnac term, neutrino-mass delay, antenna cable delays;
.IP \(bu 2
the \fBopening angle to the beam axis\fR (a global reference line between two sites), plus the
off-axis angle and transverse position of every site relative to that axis;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBopening angles\fR between two chords that share a vertex site (for example target\(->ND
against target\(->FD);
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsensitivities\fR of the chord to each input and an \fBerror budget\fR built from the
uncertainties declared for each site.
.PP
Sites may be given geodetically (latitude, longitude, height with an explicit datum), as
Fermilab site coordinates (FSCS/DUSAF feet, converted with the Beams-doc-1148 projection),
or decoded from NMEA $GPGGA sentences.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
YAML configuration.  Default: \fIconfig/gps-tof.yaml\fR, then \fIconfig/nova.yaml\fR, if present.
See \fBgps-tof\fR(5) for the format.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fISPEC\fR
Add a site from the command line.  \fISPEC\fR is a list of \fIkey=value\fR tokens with shell
quoting: \fBname\fR, \fBid\fR, \fBlat\fR, \fBlon\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBdatum\fR (ellipsoid|msl),
\fBgeoid\fR, \fBoffset\fR=E,N,U, \fBfscs\fR=x,y,z (feet), \fBnote\fR, \fBcable_m\fR, \fBvf\fR,
\fBcable_delay_ns\fR, and \fBu_\fIkey\fR for uncertainties (u_lat_arcsec, u_lon_arcsec,
u_height_m, u_offset_m, u_quantization_arcsec).  Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pair " " \fIFROM:TO[:LABEL[:AXIS]]\fR
Baseline between two site ids.  \fIAXIS\fR names a third site; the angle between
FROM\(->TO and FROM\(->AXIS is reported as the off-axis angle.  Repeatable.  Without
\fB\-p\fR and without \fBbaselines:\fR in the file, every pair of sites is computed.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-axis " " \fIORIGIN:THROUGH[:LABEL]\fR
Define the beam axis as the line from site \fIORIGIN\fR through site \fITHROUGH\fR (for NuMI,
target \(-> MINOS far detector).  Every site is then reported with its off-axis angle at the
origin, its distance along the axis and its transverse position (right/up looking downstream);
every baseline gets the opening angle between its chord and the axis and the off-axis angle of
both endpoints; every opening angle gets the angle of each chord to the axis.  Overrides
\fBbeam_axis:\fR in the file.  \fB\-\-no\-axis\fR suppresses the axis in the report (sites given with
\fBaxis_position\fR are still placed with it).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-angle " " \fIVERTEX:A:B[:LABEL[:AXIS]]\fR
Opening angle at site \fIVERTEX\fR between the chords VERTEX\(->A and VERTEX\(->B, with its
horizontal and vertical components at the vertex, the transverse distance of each endpoint
from the other chord, the difference of the two flight times, and (with \fIAXIS\fR) the
off-axis angle of each chord.  Repeatable; overrides \fBopening_angles:\fR in the file.
.TP
.BR \-\-nmea " " \fIFILE\fR
Add one site per $GPGGA sentence in \fIFILE\fR (MSL altitude plus geoid separation).
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-timing\-shifts
Ignore the \fBtiming_shifts\fR of the configuration.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " text|md|json|all
Output format (default text).  \fBall\fR writes \fIOUTPUT\fR.md, .txt and .json.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Write the report to \fIPATH\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-\-dump\-config
Print the merged configuration (file, .env, environment and command line applied) as YAML and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-list\-sites
Print the resolved sites and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
.SS Setting overrides
\fB\-\-project\fR, \fB\-\-clock\-hz\fR, \fB\-\-legacy\-unit\-s\fR, \fB\-\-earth\-model\fR wgs84|grs80|sphere,
\fB\-\-sphere\-radius\-m\fR, \fB\-\-offset\-normal\fR geodetic|radial, \fB\-\-apply\-sagnac\fR,
\fB\-\-rounding\fR round|floor|ceil, \fB\-\-precision\-digits\fR, \fB\-\-neutrino\-mass\-ev\fR,
\fB\-\-neutrino\-energy\-gev\fR, \fB\-\-window\-before\-us\fR, \fB\-\-window\-after\-us\fR,
\fB\-\-microslice\-us\fR, \fB\-\-no\-sensitivities\fR, \fB\-\-arcsec\-step\fR, \fB\-\-height\-step\-m\fR,
\fB\-\-offset\-step\-m\fR.
.SH CONFIGURATION PRECEDENCE
command line > environment (\fBGPS_TOF_\fIKEY\fR) > \fI.env\fR (current directory, then \fIconfig/.env\fR)
> configuration file > built-in defaults.  Environment keys: GPS_TOF_CONFIG, GPS_TOF_FORMAT,
GPS_TOF_OUTPUT and the upper-cased setting names (GPS_TOF_CLOCK_HZ, GPS_TOF_EARTH_MODEL, ...).
.SH EXAMPLES
Full NOvA report in Markdown:
.PP
.nf
    gps-tof -c config/nova.yaml -f md -o reports/nova.md
.fi
.PP
Two ad-hoc sites, no configuration file, 100 MHz clock:
.PP
.nf
    gps-tof -c /dev/null --clock-hz 100e6 \\
        -s 'name=A id=a lat=41:50:23N lon=88:16:13W h=228 offset=0,0,-120' \\
        -s 'name=B id=b lat="48 22.715780 N" lon="92 49.876930 W" h=348.638 offset=0,0,-19.8' \\
        -p a:b
.fi
.PP
NuMI target from Fermilab site coordinates, off-axis angle against the decay-pipe end, and the
opening angle at the target between the ND and FD chords:
.PP
.nf
    gps-tof -c config/nova.yaml -p target:fd2:"target to FD":decay-end -a target:nd:fd2::decay-end
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 2 on a usage or configuration error.
.SH FILES
.I config/nova.yaml
NOvA NuMI\(->Ash River example configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gps-tof (5),
.BR gps-tof-nmea (1),
.BR gps-tof-fscs (1),
.BR gps_tof (3)
